{
  "dom_id": "step-q183431",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${3}^{n}\\ge (n+1)^{2}$ for $n\\ge 2,n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\\ge {{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-9}}={{field-10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
